		"brief": "An interactive single-file HTML page (no external dependencies, plain canvas/SVG + vanilla JS) teaching WHICH linear maps leave pairwise geometry unchanged. Concept: a representation is only its Gram matrix; orthogonal maps preserve it exactly, and any other invertible map (including determinant-1 shears) destroys it.\n\nLAYOUT: two panels side by side, same scale. Left panel labelled \"original points\", right panel labelled \"after Q\". The SAME point cloud of 6 points in R^2 (fixed default coordinates, e.g. (1,0.5), (2,1.8), (1.6,2.4), (0.6,1.2), (2.4,0.9), (1.1,2.0) — give a small \"randomize points\" button that regenerates them within a 3x3 box, but always keep a fixed default on load). Draw faint grey lines between every pair of points in both panels so pairwise distances are visible. Points labelled A–F.\n\nCONTROLS (right of or below the panels):\n- Single rotation-angle slider theta from 0 to 360 degrees.\n- Single shear slider s from -1.5 to 1.5, matrix S = [[1, s],[0, 1]] (note det S = 1 always — display \"det = 1.00\" live next to the shear slider in a way that is prominent).\n- Scale slider k from 0.5 to 2, matrix k*I.\n- A mode selector: \"rotation\", \"shear\", \"scaling\", \"rotation x shear\" — the sliders that are inactive for the current mode are visibly greyed out. Default mode: rotation.\n- The applied 2x2 matrix Q is displayed numerically, updated live.\n- Small preset buttons: \"rotation 37 degrees\", \"shear 0.8 (det=1)\", \"det=1 rotation-then-shear\".\n\nREADOUTS (must be prominent and update live):\n1. A Gram matrix comparison: show the 6x6 Gram matrix K = X X^T for the original points, and K' = X' X'^T for the transformed points, as two small heatmaps side by side with numeric values on hover (or show just the upper triangle numerically in a compact table). Above them, a single big number: max relative change max_{i,j} |K'_ij - K_ij| / max_{i,j}|K_ij| , shown as a percentage. For rotation this must read 0.00%.\n2. A second big readout: max relative change in pairwise DISTANCES, same normalization. For rotation: 0.00%. For shear 0.8: substantially nonzero.\n3. A verdict line that reads, depending on the matrix: \"This map is orthogonal: it preserves every distance and the whole Gram matrix\" when Q^T Q is identity to numerical tolerance (1e-9), otherwise \"This map is NOT orthogonal: distances change\" — plus, in the non-orthogonal case only, a short factual line showing ||Q^T Q - I||_F and det(Q).\n\nSTYLE: clean, minimal, light background, dark text, one accent colour (blue) for the original cloud and a second (orange) for the transformed cloud. Readable at projector size. No emoji.\n\nIMPORTANT: state in a caption under the controls, in plain words, that det(Q) = 1 does not imply distances are preserved, and that the shear preset is the counterexample. Ensure everything runs with no console errors.",
